{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EkiBe72g126JKXBDq1n7Cg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd087-sizeparse\n2\ttitle: \"pkg/sizeparse: Size String Parser with Unit Suffixes\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Eight cmd/ utilities (head, tail, split, truncate, numfmt, du, sort, od) need to parse\n6\t  size strings with unit suffixes (K, M, G, T for powers of 1024; KB, MB, GB for powers\n7\t  of 1000; b for 512). In run 37, each utility independently implemented suffix parsing\n8\t  with slight variations, totaling ~50 lines per utility. This duplication risks\n9\t  inconsistent suffix handling across utilities.\n10\t\n11\t  pkg/sizeparse provides a single parser that handles all GNU coreutils suffix\n12\t  conventions. Each cmd/ utility imports this package instead of reimplementing suffix\n13\t  multiplication.\n14\t\n15\tgoals:\n16\t  - G1: Parse size strings with binary (IEC) and decimal (SI) unit suffixes.\n17\t  - G2: Support the full GNU coreutils suffix set including b, K, KiB, KB, M, MiB, MB, G, GiB, GB, T, TiB, TB, P, PiB, PB, E, EiB, EB, Z, ZiB, ZB, Y, YiB, YB.\n18\t  - G3: Support optional sign prefixes (+, -) for relative size adjustments.\n19\t\n20\trequirements:\n21\t  R1:\n22\t    title: Size parsing\n23\t    items:\n24\t      - R1.1:\n25\t          text: |\n26\t            Must provide func Parse(s string) (int64, error) that parses a size string\n27\t            consisting of an optional sign (+ or -), a decimal integer, and an optional\n28\t            unit suffix. Returns the size in bytes. Returns an error for invalid input.\n29\t      - R1.2:\n30\t          text: |\n31\t            Must support binary (IEC) suffixes: K or KiB (1024), M or MiB (1024^2),\n32\t            G or GiB (1024^3), T or TiB (1024^4), P or PiB (1024^5), E or EiB (1024^6),\n33\t            Z or ZiB (1024^7), Y or YiB (1024^8). Must also support b (512) for\n34\t            POSIX compatibility.\n35\t      - R1.3:\n36\t          text: |\n37\t            Must support decimal (SI) suffixes: KB (1000), MB (1000^2), GB (1000^3),\n38\t            TB (1000^4), PB (1000^5), EB (1000^6), ZB (1000^7), YB (1000^8).\n39\t      - R1.4:\n40\t          text: Must preserve the sign in the returned value. \"+100K\" returns +102400, \"-50M\" returns -52428800. No sign prefix means positive.\n41\t\n42\t  R2:\n43\t    title: Extended parsing options\n44\t    items:\n45\t      - R2.1:\n46\t          text: |\n47\t            Must provide func ParseWithOptions(s string, opts ParseOptions) (int64, error)\n48\t            where ParseOptions allows configuring behavior:\n49\t\n50\t              type ParseOptions struct {\n51\t                  AllowSign    bool // allow +/- prefix (default false for Parse)\n52\t                  DefaultUnit  int64 // multiplier when no suffix given (default 1)\n53\t              }\n54\t      - R2.2:\n55\t          text: \"ParseWithOptions with DefaultUnit=1024 and input '5' returns 5120 (5 * 1024). This supports utilities like du where the default unit is blocks.\"\n56\t\n57\t  R3:\n58\t    title: Constraints\n59\t    items:\n60\t      - R3.1:\n61\t          text: Must return an error when the numeric part overflows int64.\n62\t      - R3.2:\n63\t          text: Must return an error when the suffix is not recognized.\n64\t\n65\tnon_goals:\n66\t  - pkg/sizeparse does not format sizes for output; use pkg/format.HumanSize for that.\n67\t  - pkg/sizeparse does not handle floating-point sizes.\n68\t\n69\tpackage_contract:\n70\t  exports:\n71\t    - name: ParseOptions\n72\t      signature: \"type ParseOptions struct { AllowSign bool; DefaultUnit int64 }\"\n73\t    - name: Parse\n74\t      signature: \"func Parse(s string) (int64, error)\"\n75\t    - name: ParseWithOptions\n76\t      signature: \"func ParseWithOptions(s string, opts ParseOptions) (int64, error)\"\n77\t  imports:\n78\t    - fmt\n79\t    - strconv\n80\t    - strings\n81\t  import_constraints:\n82\t    - \"Must not import any cmd/ package.\"\n83\t    - \"Must not import any other pkg/ package.\"\n84\t\n85\tacceptance_criteria:\n86\t  - id: AC1\n87\t    criterion: \"Parse('100K') returns 102400.\"\n88\t    traces:\n89\t      - R1.1\n90\t      - R1.2\n91\t  - id: AC2\n92\t    criterion: \"Parse('5MB') returns 5000000.\"\n93\t    traces:\n94\t      - R1.1\n95\t      - R1.3\n96\t  - id: AC3\n97\t    criterion: \"ParseWithOptions('+50M', ParseOptions{AllowSign: true}) returns 52428800.\"\n98\t    traces:\n99\t      - R1.4\n100\t      - R2.1\n101\t  - id: AC4\n102\t    criterion: \"Parse('invalid') returns an error.\"\n103\t    traces:\n104\t      - R3.2\n105\t  - id: AC5\n106\t    criterion: \"pkg/sizeparse compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n107\t    traces:\n108\t      - R1.1\n109\t      - R1.2\n110\t      - R1.3\n111\t      - R1.4\n112\t      - R2.1\n113\t      - R2.2\n114\t      - R3.1\n115\t      - R3.2\n116\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5b4aa089-8057-4e95-ae15-61ff1264306f","uuid":"7ff9f347-177a-49b1-bb64-8a45b2ab1803","timestamp":"2026-05-13T22:48:27.886Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5082/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd087-sizeparse.yaml","content":"id: srd087-sizeparse\ntitle: \"pkg/sizeparse: Size String Parser with Unit Suffixes\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Eight cmd/ utilities (head, tail, split, truncate, numfmt, du, sort, od) need to parse\n  size strings with unit suffixes (K, M, G, T for powers of 1024; KB, MB, GB for powers\n  of 1000; b for 512). In run 37, each utility independently implemented suffix parsing\n  with slight variations, totaling ~50 lines per utility. This duplication risks\n  inconsistent suffix handling across utilities.\n\n  pkg/sizeparse provides a single parser that handles all GNU coreutils suffix\n  conventions. Each cmd/ utility imports this package instead of reimplementing suffix\n  multiplication.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Parse size strings with binary (IEC) and decimal (SI) unit suffixes.\n  - G2: Support the full GNU coreutils suffix set including b, K, KiB, KB, M, MiB, MB, G, GiB, GB, T, TiB, TB, P, PiB, PB, E, EiB, EB, Z, ZiB, ZB, Y, YiB, YB.\n  - G3: Support optional sign prefixes (+, -) for relative size adjustments.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Size parsing\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func Parse(s string) (int64, error) that parses a size string\n            consisting of an optional sign (+ or -), a decimal integer, and an optional\n            unit suffix. Returns the size in bytes. Returns an error for invalid input.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: |\n            Must support binary (IEC) suffixes: K or KiB (1024), M or MiB (1024^2),\n            G or GiB (1024^3), T or TiB (1024^4), P or PiB (1024^5), E or EiB (1024^6),\n            Z or ZiB (1024^7), Y or YiB (1024^8). Must also support b (512) for\n            POSIX compatibility.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: |\n            Must support decimal (SI) suffixes: KB (1000), MB (1000^2), GB (1000^3),\n            TB (1000^4), PB (1000^5), EB (1000^6), ZB (1000^7), YB (1000^8).\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must preserve the sign in the returned value. \"+100K\" returns +102400, \"-50M\" returns -52428800. No sign prefix means positive.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Extended parsing options\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func ParseWithOptions(s string, opts ParseOptions) (int64, error)\n            where ParseOptions allows configuring behavior:\n\n              type ParseOptions struct {\n                  AllowSign    bool // allow +/- prefix (default false for Parse)\n                  DefaultUnit  int64 // multiplier when no suffix given (default 1)\n              }\n      - R2.2:\n          text: \"ParseWithOptions with DefaultUnit=1024 and input '5' returns 5120 (5 * 1024). This supports utilities like du where the default unit is blocks.\"\n\n  R3:\n    title: Constraints\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: Must return an error when the numeric part overflows int64.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: Must return an error when the suffix is not recognized.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/sizeparse does not format sizes for output; use pkg/format.HumanSize for that.\n  - pkg/sizeparse does not handle floating-point sizes.\n\npackage_contract:\n  exports:\n    - name: ParseOptions\n      signature: \"type ParseOptions struct { AllowSign bool; DefaultUnit int64 }\"\n    - name: Parse\n      signature: \"func Parse(s string) (int64, error)\"\n    - name: ParseWithOptions\n      signature: \"func ParseWithOptions(s string, opts ParseOptions) (int64, error)\"\n  imports:\n    - fmt\n    - strconv\n    - strings\n  import_constraints:\n    - \"Must not import any cmd/ package.\"\n    - \"Must not import any other pkg/ package.\"\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"Parse('100K') returns 102400.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"Parse('5MB') returns 5000000.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.3\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"ParseWithOptions('+50M', ParseOptions{AllowSign: true}) returns 52428800.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"Parse('invalid') returns an error.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.2\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"pkg/sizeparse compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n","numLines":116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":116}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CiiRfF43t4mvYx9vSE8Xhb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the requirements. Let me review the existing code from the project_context:\n\nThe existing `pkg/sizeparse/parse.go` already has:\n- `ParseOptions` struct with `AllowSign` and `DefaultUnit` fields\n- `Parse()` function that delegates to `ParseWithOptions` with default options\n- `ParseWithOptions()` that handles sign, numeric part, suffix parsing\n- Suffix maps for binary/decimal/overflow suffixes\n- `splitParts()` and `resolveMultiplier()` helper functions\n\nLooking at the requirements I need to implement:\n- **R2.1**: `ParseWithOptions` with configurable `AllowSign` and `DefaultUnit` - this is ALREADY implemented in the existing code\n\n\n- **R2.2**: `ParseWithOptions` with `DefaultUnit=1024` and input `'5'` returns `5120` - this is ALREADY implemented via `resolveMultiplier` which uses `defaultUnit` when suffix is empty\n- **R3.1**: Must return error when numeric part overflows int64 - the existing code uses `strconv.ParseInt` which already handles int64 overflow, but multiplication overflow is NOT checked\n- **R3.2**: Must return error when suffix is not recognized - this is ALREADY implemented in `resolveMultiplier`\n\nSo the main gap is handling multiplication overflow in R3.1. I need to add overflow checking when multiplying the parsed number by the multiplier. error handling\". Let me look more carefully at what might be missing or need improvement:\n\n1. **R2.1**: The `ParseWithOptions` function exists and `ParseOptions` struct has both fields. The behavior seems correct - `AllowSign` controls whether signs are accepted, `DefaultUnit` provides the multiplier when no suffix is given. This looks complete.\n\n2. **R2.2**: The `resolveMultiplier` function handles the case where `suffix == \"\"` and `defaultUnit != 0` by returning `defaultUnit`. So `ParseWithOptions(\"5\", ParseOptions{DefaultUnit: 1024})` would call `resolveMultiplier(\"\", 1024)` which returns `1024`, then `5 * 1024 = 5120`. This looks correct.\n\n3. **R3.1**: The numeric parsing via `strconv.ParseInt(numStr, 10, 64)` will catch overflow in the numeric part. But I'm realizing there's a gap - what about overflow from multiplying `n * multiplier`? For example, `Parse(\"9999999999999999999E\")` where the numeric part is valid but the multiplication overflows int64. The current code doesn't check for this, so I need to add overflow detection for the multiplication step.\n\n4. **R3.2**: The `resolveMultiplier` function already returns an error for unrecognized suffixes. This is implemented.\n\nNow looking at the sign handling more carefully - the code extracts the sign prefix and applies it to the result, but I need to verify the logic is correct throughout the entire calculation.\n\nThere's actually a conflict I'm noticing between R1.1 and R2.1. R1.1 says `Parse` accepts an optional sign, but R2.1 says `AllowSign` defaults to false for `Parse`. Let me check R1.4 which says the sign must be preserved in the returned value.\n\nLooking at the acceptance criteria, AC3 uses `ParseWithOptions` with `AllowSign: true` explicitly, not `Parse` directly. So it seems `Parse` without options doesn't allow signs by default, while `ParseWithOptions` with the flag enabled does. The requirements describe the general format, but the actual sign handling is controlled by the `AllowSign` option.\n\nNow I need to focus on what actually needs fixing. `ParseWithOptions` already handles `AllowSign` and `DefaultUnit` correctly per R2.1 and R2.2. But for R3.1, I need to add overflow detection when multiplying `n * multiplier` since large values could overflow int64.\n\nI also need to check if `strings` is imported—the contract specifies `fmt, strconv, strings` but the current code only has `fmt` and `strconv`. I might not need to add it if it's not being used.\n\nThe key changes are adding overflow detection for the multiplication operation, and possibly for negation when the result is `math.MinInt64`. The numeric parsing itself is already handled by `strconv.ParseInt`, but the subsequent multiplication can still overflow. The main gap is handling multiplication overflow when applying the unit multiplier, which R3.1 requires. I should add that check to `ParseWithOptions`, and then verify the overall implementation covers all the requirements—particularly that `Parse` correctly defaults `AllowSign` to false as specified.\n\nFor the overflow detection, since both the parsed number and multiplier are positive, I need to check if `n > math.MaxInt64/multiplier` before multiplying. The tricky part is handling the sign afterward—applying negation could theoretically overflow if the result is `math.MinInt64`, but that's an edge case I should account for.\n\nAdding overflow detection... I'm realizing the import constraints explicitly limit me to fmt, strconv, and strings, so I can't use the math package—I'll just define MaxInt64 as a constant instead.\n\nWriting the overflow check... Adding overflow detection...\n\nI'm verifying the logic handles all cases correctly: when `DefaultUnit` is 0, the multiplier defaults to 1, which means bare numbers are treated as bytes with no unit scaling applied, matching the expected behavior for `Parse(\"5\")` returning 5 and `ParseWithOptions(\"5\", ParseOptions{DefaultUnit: 1024})` returning 5120.\n\nThe error handling for unrecognized and overflow suffixes is already in place through the `resolveMultiplier` function, so I just need to add the `maxInt64` constant and the overflow check to complete the implementation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1761,"cache_read_input_tokens":156455,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1761},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_